  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM MDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nOver the past six weeks or so, the main weather story has been one \nof two things: heat, some record breaking; and the monsoonal \nmoisture which as stuck around a long time and is wearing out\nits welcome like an house guest that won't leave. Some days it \nwas one or the other, other days it was both. And the next seven\ndays will be no exception.\n\nToday looks rather active. Dew points are rising across the area as \nanother plume of the moisture moves into the area. One difference \ntoday is we will have a subtle shortwave moving across the area, \nproviding some additional lift. Just about everyone will have a \nchance of a shower or thunderstorms today, but, much like many of \nthe previous days, not everyone will see one. As for threats, there \ncould be heavy rain with any storm, with precipitable water values \nrising close to an inch in some locations, which approaches 175 \npercent of normal. However, we do have decent wind at the mid \nlevels, approaching 40 knots in some areas. So these storms will \nhave movement, and this should mitigate the threat of flooding \nsomewhat. However, we do have some other possible threats today. We \ndo have a marginal risk of severe weather roughly along and east of \na Cody to Shoshoni to Rawlins and eastward. The main threat looks to \nbe strong wind. Dew point depressions are not all that large \n(generally 30 to 40 degrees), however lapse rates are rather steep \n(over 8C/Km). Sounding also have somewhat of an inverted V signature.\nHodographs are also largely straight with wind fairly well aligned.\nWith the moist atmosphere, I also can't rule out some hail, but\nthe threat of this smaller. Western portions of state look to have\nthe most storms during the afternoon, with the greatest coverage\nshifting to the east in the evening. And a few storms could linger\nwell into the afternoon before ending after midnight. As for \ntemperatures, they should run fairly similar to yesterday, \nfairly close to seasonal averages.\n\nThen, for the next three days or so, the main concern shifts toward \ntemperatures. Almost all guidance shows the monsoonal moisture \ngetting suppressed to the south and east as high pressure over the \ndesert southwest builds northward. This will lead to temperatures \ngoing up and the chances of showers and storms dropping. At this \npoint, Wednesday looks like the driest day as we finally get a day \nof below normal precipitable water across most of the area. We can't\ncompletely rule out a stray shower or thunderstorm across far \nsouthern Sweetwater County, but even here the chance is less than 1 \nout of 6. With the ridge building back northward, 700 millibar \ntemperatures will be climbing again, likely peaking on Friday when \nsome areas each 20 celsius. This will lead to a period of above \nnormal temperatures starting on Wednesday and peaking on Friday. Most \nareas East of the Divide will likely see highs in the 90s starting \non Thursday and lasting through at least Saturday. Friday looks to \nbe peak of the heat with most locations with elevations less than \n5000 feet having at least a 1 in 2 chance of highs of 95 or over. A \nbit more moisture may return on Thursday or Friday, but almost \nall convection on these days will be confined to the western \nmountains and chances will be rather low, generally less than 1 \nout of 4 each afternoon at any particular location.\n\nConcerns then shift to a another return of the monsoon for the \nweekend and into next week. Recent models have slowed the return of \nthe moisture a bit for Saturday, with precipitable water values \ngenerally 20 percent less than yesterday. The current forecast for \nFriday night is likely too wet, the deepest moisture looks to return \nSaturday and into Sunday and early next week. There is some \ndisagreement in the guidance in regards to the details of the \nmost active day of the monsoon as well as placement of the most \nnumerous convection, as would be expected this far out. However,\nprecipitable water values look to remain above normal for much \nof next week. Temperatures should also lower somewhat with more\ncloud cover, but will likely remain near to somewhat above \nnormal through the period. So, if I could sum up how the next \nweek has compared to much of the summer, it would be more of the\nsame.",
